1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:03,260
Hello everyone, and welcome to Two Cups English.

2
00:00:03,840 --> 00:00:06,720
I'm Mia, and Ben is here with me.

3
00:00:06,780 --> 00:00:10,160
This podcast is for people who are learning English.

4
00:00:10,220 --> 00:00:11,050
We speak slowly.

5
00:00:11,640 --> 00:00:12,970
We use simple words.

6
00:00:13,560 --> 00:00:17,300
And we repeat the important words many times.

7
00:00:17,360 --> 00:00:19,680
Today, our topic is words.

8
00:00:19,780 --> 00:00:21,300
Learning one word a day.

9
00:00:22,260 --> 00:00:23,980
Ben, what is your word for today?

10
00:00:24,960 --> 00:00:25,420
Hi Mia.

11
00:00:25,860 --> 00:00:26,480
Hi everyone.

12
00:00:27,560 --> 00:00:30,520
My word today is "shelf," Mia.

13
00:00:31,680 --> 00:00:32,280
In Spanish.

14
00:00:33,170 --> 00:00:41,540
"Estante." Because yesterday, in a shop, I wanted to ask where the rice was, and I did not have the word.

15
00:00:42,860 --> 00:00:43,600
I pointed.

16
00:00:44,100 --> 00:00:44,820
Like a child.

17
00:00:45,700 --> 00:00:46,620
My wife laughed.

18
00:00:47,760 --> 00:00:48,320
What about you?

19
00:00:48,840 --> 00:00:49,540
Do you have a word?

20
00:00:50,800 --> 00:00:51,820
Not one word, Ben.

21
00:00:52,300 --> 00:00:53,500
Three thousand.

22
00:00:54,220 --> 00:00:56,240
When I was learning Spanish, I had a list.

23
00:00:56,830 --> 00:01:02,180
"The 3,000 most important Spanish words." And I learned fifty words every night.

24
00:01:02,960 --> 00:01:03,250
Fifty.

25
00:01:03,880 --> 00:01:05,099
For two months.

26
00:01:06,040 --> 00:01:07,940
And at the end, I did a test.

27
00:01:08,660 --> 00:01:10,640
I could remember about two hundred.

28
00:01:11,380 --> 00:01:13,700
Two hundred, from three thousand.

29
00:01:14,460 --> 00:01:15,260
And I cried.

30
00:01:16,300 --> 00:01:23,390
"Two hundred, from three thousand." Mia, two months of nights, and the words were gone.

31
00:01:24,720 --> 00:01:27,120
That is our topic today.

32
00:01:27,200 --> 00:01:28,440
So here is our plan.

33
00:01:29,460 --> 00:01:32,460
In part one, we talk about our own lists.

34
00:01:33,600 --> 00:01:38,380
In part two, we ask why words disappear, and how they stay.

35
00:01:39,250 --> 00:01:43,070
And I have a true story for that, from 1885.

36
00:01:44,200 --> 00:01:48,230
In part three, we share simple ways to learn one word a day.

37
00:01:49,360 --> 00:01:50,140
Does that sound good?

38
00:01:51,040 --> 00:01:52,040
That sounds good, Ben.

39
00:01:52,700 --> 00:01:56,220
And to everyone listening, please do not worry if you miss something.

40
00:01:56,740 --> 00:01:57,820
You will hear it again.

41
00:01:58,620 --> 00:02:02,260
And today, "you will hear it again" is not only a promise.

42
00:02:02,880 --> 00:02:04,100
It is the whole topic.

43
00:02:04,880 --> 00:02:07,720
Some words will come back many times.

44
00:02:07,820 --> 00:02:08,500
Okay, Ben.

45
00:02:08,560 --> 00:02:09,039
Part one.

46
00:02:09,860 --> 00:02:10,940
Did you have a list too?

47
00:02:12,260 --> 00:02:13,440
The same list, Mia.

48
00:02:14,280 --> 00:02:15,760
In English, when I was young.

49
00:02:16,640 --> 00:02:21,360
Different language, same list, same fifty a night, same tears.

50
00:02:22,440 --> 00:02:24,800
And for years, I thought I went wrong.

51
00:02:25,540 --> 00:02:26,160
My memory.

52
00:02:26,540 --> 00:02:27,160
My brain.

53
00:02:27,970 --> 00:02:31,520
"I am not a language person." Did you think that?

54
00:02:32,760 --> 00:02:34,100
That was my sentence, Ben.

55
00:02:34,700 --> 00:02:38,140
"I am not a language person." For years.

56
00:02:38,930 --> 00:02:42,340
But the list did not go wrong, and my brain did not go wrong.

57
00:02:42,950 --> 00:02:44,049
The method went wrong.

58
00:02:44,980 --> 00:02:48,880
Fifty words in one night is like eating fifty meals in one night.

59
00:02:49,510 --> 00:02:50,840
The body cannot use it.

60
00:02:51,440 --> 00:02:52,120
It goes through.

61
00:02:52,860 --> 00:02:54,440
Fifty meals, one night.

62
00:02:55,160 --> 00:02:57,040
You cannot remember fifty meals.

63
00:02:58,060 --> 00:02:59,160
It goes through.

64
00:03:00,380 --> 00:03:03,080
Mia, let me ask something about those two months.

65
00:03:04,020 --> 00:03:05,900
Did it feel like learning, at the time?

66
00:03:06,780 --> 00:03:07,340
Be honest.

67
00:03:08,340 --> 00:03:09,580
It felt like learning, Ben.

68
00:03:10,240 --> 00:03:11,220
That is the trap.

69
00:03:11,980 --> 00:03:16,980
Every night, fifty words, and at the end of the hour, I could remember all fifty.

70
00:03:17,820 --> 00:03:19,780
I closed the list and I felt full.

71
00:03:20,500 --> 00:03:23,040
I felt like a person who had learned fifty words.

72
00:03:23,660 --> 00:03:24,320
And I had.

73
00:03:24,840 --> 00:03:25,609
For one hour.

74
00:03:26,480 --> 00:03:27,680
For one hour, Mia.

75
00:03:28,360 --> 00:03:29,620
The feeling was real.

76
00:03:30,520 --> 00:03:31,990
The learning was not.

77
00:03:32,720 --> 00:03:35,470
And I think that is why the list is so popular.

78
00:03:35,480 --> 00:03:37,260
It gives the feeling every night.

79
00:03:38,020 --> 00:03:39,500
Seeing is not learning.

80
00:03:40,220 --> 00:03:42,000
But seeing feels like learning.

81
00:03:42,700 --> 00:03:43,500
Not for a week.

82
00:03:44,260 --> 00:03:47,110
And remembering for one hour is not remembering.

83
00:03:48,010 --> 00:03:52,960
"Seeing is not learning." Ben, that should be on the first page of the list.

84
00:03:53,000 --> 00:03:55,410
And there is something else from my two hundred words.

85
00:03:55,940 --> 00:03:57,320
The ones I still remember.

86
00:03:57,940 --> 00:03:58,979
I looked at them later.

87
00:03:59,500 --> 00:04:01,700
They were not the first two hundred on the list.

88
00:04:02,520 --> 00:04:03,700
Not the first two hundred, Mia?

89
00:04:04,620 --> 00:04:07,780
I would have guessed the first ones, the easy ones.

90
00:04:07,820 --> 00:04:08,830
Then which ones stayed?

91
00:04:09,489 --> 00:04:10,260
The ones you needed?

92
00:04:10,949 --> 00:04:12,300
The words I heard again, Ben.

93
00:04:12,680 --> 00:04:13,680
In songs.

94
00:04:13,740 --> 00:04:14,360
In the shop.

95
00:04:14,800 --> 00:04:15,400
In a film.

96
00:04:15,970 --> 00:04:21,900
"Pan." "Agua." "Gracias." Words that life said to me again, after the list.

97
00:04:22,540 --> 00:04:23,900
The list said them once.

98
00:04:24,380 --> 00:04:25,500
Life said them again.

99
00:04:26,120 --> 00:04:27,640
And the again is what stayed.

100
00:04:28,080 --> 00:04:29,360
Again and again.

101
00:04:29,960 --> 00:04:31,940
I remember them because I met them again.

102
00:04:32,710 --> 00:04:34,720
"The list said them once.

103
00:04:34,800 --> 00:04:40,420
Life said them again." Mia, that is the whole episode in two sentences.

104
00:04:40,440 --> 00:04:45,120
But let me tell you about my father before part two, because he did this without science.

105
00:04:45,980 --> 00:04:48,640
He came to this country at forty, with no English.

106
00:04:49,520 --> 00:04:51,520
At forty, Ben, with no English?

107
00:04:52,200 --> 00:04:54,450
How did he learn, with a job and a family?

108
00:04:55,340 --> 00:04:57,060
One word a day, Mia.

109
00:04:57,100 --> 00:04:58,520
He told me this when I was small.

110
00:04:59,490 --> 00:05:00,260
"One word, Ben.

111
00:05:00,860 --> 00:05:01,330
Every day.

112
00:05:02,320 --> 00:05:03,260
Then I use it at work.

113
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:05,080
Then I use it at home.

114
00:05:05,940 --> 00:05:07,100
Then I remember it.

115
00:05:07,880 --> 00:05:12,040
Then it is mine." One word, used three times.

116
00:05:12,860 --> 00:05:14,440
And he did that for thirty years.

117
00:05:15,190 --> 00:05:15,760
Do the math.

118
00:05:16,520 --> 00:05:18,160
Ten thousand words, Ben.

119
00:05:18,200 --> 00:05:19,420
From one a day.

120
00:05:19,480 --> 00:05:21,120
That is more than my list.

121
00:05:21,160 --> 00:05:22,720
And he spoke well?

122
00:05:23,580 --> 00:05:24,900
Better than me, in the end, Mia.

123
00:05:25,540 --> 00:05:27,960
With an accent, and with ten thousand words.

124
00:05:28,700 --> 00:05:30,800
He never had a list, and he had no time.

125
00:05:31,500 --> 00:05:33,520
He had a day, and one word from it.

126
00:05:34,480 --> 00:05:36,040
Okay, so that was part one.

127
00:05:36,600 --> 00:05:37,220
Our lists.

128
00:05:38,160 --> 00:05:44,150
Three thousand words, fifty a night, two hundred that stayed, and a father with one word a day.

129
00:05:45,220 --> 00:05:46,980
Now, part two.

130
00:05:47,040 --> 00:05:49,430
Why do words disappear, and how do they stay?

131
00:05:50,360 --> 00:05:53,330
Mia, my true story is from 1885.

132
00:05:54,320 --> 00:05:55,680
1885, Ben?

133
00:05:56,310 --> 00:05:59,920
Nobody was learning English from a podcast in 1885.

134
00:06:00,520 --> 00:06:02,760
Who is the story about, and what did he do?

135
00:06:03,500 --> 00:06:04,300
A German, Mia.

136
00:06:05,160 --> 00:06:06,580
Okay, part two.

137
00:06:06,660 --> 00:06:08,720
This is a true story.

138
00:06:08,780 --> 00:06:10,280
His name was Hermann Ebbinghaus.

139
00:06:10,950 --> 00:06:12,780
And he wanted to know how forgetting works.

140
00:06:13,689 --> 00:06:15,380
So he did something strange.

141
00:06:15,440 --> 00:06:16,380
He made up words.

142
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:17,880
Nonsense words.

143
00:06:18,610 --> 00:06:23,240
"Zof." "Wub." "Kem." Hundreds of them.

144
00:06:23,270 --> 00:06:24,480
And he learned them.

145
00:06:24,510 --> 00:06:25,030
Himself.

146
00:06:25,730 --> 00:06:27,300
For years.

147
00:06:27,380 --> 00:06:29,320
He learned made-up words for years, Ben?

148
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:31,990
Why not real words, like bread and water?

149
00:06:32,860 --> 00:06:35,780
Because real words have meaning, Mia, and meaning helps.

150
00:06:36,300 --> 00:06:37,610
He wanted words with no help.

151
00:06:38,140 --> 00:06:41,599
So he learned a list of these sounds until he could say them perfectly.

152
00:06:41,640 --> 00:06:42,860
And then he waited.

153
00:06:43,960 --> 00:06:44,460
One hour.

154
00:06:45,120 --> 00:06:45,620
One day.

155
00:06:46,400 --> 00:06:46,900
One week.

156
00:06:47,680 --> 00:06:49,319
And he tested how much he could remember.

157
00:06:49,880 --> 00:06:51,300
And he drew a line on paper.

158
00:06:51,980 --> 00:06:52,760
A line, Ben?

159
00:06:53,220 --> 00:06:53,770
On paper?

160
00:06:54,780 --> 00:06:57,460
I did not know forgetting could be drawn.

161
00:06:57,560 --> 00:06:59,220
What did the line show?

162
00:06:59,280 --> 00:07:00,560
The line went down, Mia.

163
00:07:00,980 --> 00:07:01,380
Fast.

164
00:07:01,940 --> 00:07:04,260
After one hour, he had lost a lot.

165
00:07:04,340 --> 00:07:06,700
After one day, he remembered less than half.

166
00:07:07,360 --> 00:07:09,680
After a week, almost nothing.

167
00:07:09,720 --> 00:07:11,240
He called it the forgetting curve.

168
00:07:11,760 --> 00:07:13,440
And it was the same every time.

169
00:07:14,100 --> 00:07:16,300
Learn, and then fall down the curve.

170
00:07:16,980 --> 00:07:18,960
More than half in one day, Ben.

171
00:07:19,540 --> 00:07:20,820
That is fast.

172
00:07:21,540 --> 00:07:24,460
Did he find anything that slowed the line down?

173
00:07:24,600 --> 00:07:26,309
Or does everything fall like that?

174
00:07:26,920 --> 00:07:29,220
Everything falls like that, Mia, the first time.

175
00:07:29,840 --> 00:07:33,700
Real words, nonsense words, names, phone numbers.

176
00:07:33,740 --> 00:07:35,480
The first night is the same for everyone.

177
00:07:36,070 --> 00:07:38,620
That is why fifty words in one night can never work.

178
00:07:39,160 --> 00:07:41,220
The curve does not care how hard you tried.

179
00:07:41,720 --> 00:07:43,640
It only cares how many times you came back.

180
00:07:44,900 --> 00:07:46,200
The curve took them, Ben.

181
00:07:47,000 --> 00:07:49,460
So my fifty words were not learned badly.

182
00:07:50,030 --> 00:07:54,160
They were learned, and then they fell down the curve, the same night.

183
00:07:54,880 --> 00:07:55,860
Exactly, Mia.

184
00:07:55,890 --> 00:07:56,730
The same night.

185
00:07:57,380 --> 00:08:00,200
But Ebbinghaus found a second thing, and this is the good part.

186
00:08:00,800 --> 00:08:04,760
When he learned the list again, the second time, he remembered it much faster.

187
00:08:05,430 --> 00:08:07,120
The words were not completely gone.

188
00:08:07,460 --> 00:08:08,180
They were sleeping.

189
00:08:08,760 --> 00:08:11,660
And when he saw them again, they woke up quickly.

190
00:08:11,670 --> 00:08:15,920
"The words were sleeping, not dead." Ben, that is kind.

191
00:08:16,550 --> 00:08:17,580
And the third time?

192
00:08:18,200 --> 00:08:19,490
Did the line keep falling?

193
00:08:20,180 --> 00:08:23,840
The third time, a few days later, the line fell more slowly, Mia.

194
00:08:24,490 --> 00:08:26,360
And the fourth time, slower again.

195
00:08:27,140 --> 00:08:32,080
And after about five meetings, on different days, the line almost stopped falling.

196
00:08:32,160 --> 00:08:33,740
He remembered almost all of it.

197
00:08:34,420 --> 00:08:38,300
Five times on five different days is close to forever.

198
00:08:38,400 --> 00:08:40,919
Five times on five different days, Ben.

199
00:08:41,780 --> 00:08:44,540
That is the opposite of fifty times in one night.

200
00:08:45,130 --> 00:08:45,929
Same fifty.

201
00:08:46,450 --> 00:08:47,329
Different days.

202
00:08:48,060 --> 00:08:50,570
And the day is the thing, not the number.

203
00:08:51,290 --> 00:08:54,960
"The day is the thing, not the number." Yes, Mia.

204
00:08:55,000 --> 00:08:56,720
So here is the whole idea of today.

205
00:08:57,680 --> 00:08:58,800
One word a day.

206
00:08:58,860 --> 00:09:00,000
Not fifty.

207
00:09:00,060 --> 00:09:00,320
One.

208
00:09:01,060 --> 00:09:04,860
And you meet that one word again, on purpose, over the next week.

209
00:09:05,540 --> 00:09:08,300
Because one word, met five times, is learned.

210
00:09:09,000 --> 00:09:12,020
Fifty words, met once, are seen.

211
00:09:12,049 --> 00:09:17,720
And a year of one word a day is three hundred and sixty-five words, all learned, Ben.

212
00:09:18,280 --> 00:09:19,030
Not seen.

213
00:09:19,700 --> 00:09:20,170
Learned.

214
00:09:20,940 --> 00:09:22,580
That is more than my two hundred.

215
00:09:23,080 --> 00:09:24,700
From one a day.

216
00:09:24,740 --> 00:09:26,670
The slow way is the fast way.

217
00:09:27,480 --> 00:09:35,560
"The slow way is the fast way." Mia, let me say one thing about the word "learn," because I had it wrong for years.

218
00:09:36,540 --> 00:09:39,270
I thought learning was the moment I understood something.

219
00:09:39,910 --> 00:09:40,390
The click.

220
00:09:41,130 --> 00:09:45,780
"Ah, shelf means estante." That click felt like learning.

221
00:09:45,840 --> 00:09:47,460
And it is not, Ben?

222
00:09:47,520 --> 00:09:48,770
The click is not learning?

223
00:09:49,480 --> 00:09:53,860
I have lived on that click for years, and it never felt like forgetting.

224
00:09:53,940 --> 00:09:57,680
Ebbinghaus showed that the click is the start of forgetting, Mia.

225
00:09:57,700 --> 00:09:58,810
Not the end of learning.

226
00:09:59,510 --> 00:10:01,280
The click is the top of the curve.

227
00:10:02,140 --> 00:10:04,919
Learning is what you do after the click.

228
00:10:04,980 --> 00:10:06,540
The again.

229
00:10:06,620 --> 00:10:10,140
So I learn a word in a week, not in a second.

230
00:10:10,180 --> 00:10:13,440
And I remember it in a week, not in a second.

231
00:10:13,480 --> 00:10:19,460
"I learn a word in a week, not in a second." Ben, that changes what I do after the click.

232
00:10:20,140 --> 00:10:21,960
Before, the click was the end.

233
00:10:22,380 --> 00:10:23,320
I closed the list.

234
00:10:24,060 --> 00:10:25,640
Now the click is the beginning.

235
00:10:26,420 --> 00:10:28,080
And it makes me kinder to myself.

236
00:10:28,720 --> 00:10:30,960
If a word falls, it is not my brain.

237
00:10:31,260 --> 00:10:32,120
It is the curve.

238
00:10:32,540 --> 00:10:33,540
Everyone's curve.

239
00:10:34,060 --> 00:10:35,360
Everyone's curve, Mia.

240
00:10:35,960 --> 00:10:37,260
Even Ebbinghaus's curve.

241
00:10:38,180 --> 00:10:39,300
And one more thing from him.

242
00:10:40,120 --> 00:10:44,740
He read his old lists years later, and the forgotten words came back fast.

243
00:10:45,460 --> 00:10:46,240
Not from zero.

244
00:10:47,000 --> 00:10:48,980
He called it saving.

245
00:10:49,020 --> 00:10:51,190
The second learning is cheaper than the first.

246
00:10:52,080 --> 00:10:54,920
So your three thousand words are not gone.

247
00:10:54,960 --> 00:10:55,880
They are asleep.

248
00:10:56,460 --> 00:10:58,940
My three thousand are asleep, Ben.

249
00:10:59,060 --> 00:10:59,940
I thought they were dead.

250
00:11:00,780 --> 00:11:03,180
One meeting, and some of them will remember me.

251
00:11:04,620 --> 00:11:06,020
Okay, so that was part two.

252
00:11:06,340 --> 00:11:07,640
Why words disappear.

253
00:11:08,540 --> 00:11:10,860
We talked about Ebbinghaus and the forgetting curve.

254
00:11:11,400 --> 00:11:13,660
We said the words are sleeping, not dead.

255
00:11:14,320 --> 00:11:18,220
And we said five meetings on five days is close to forever.

256
00:11:19,040 --> 00:11:20,140
Now, part three.

257
00:11:20,840 --> 00:11:23,200
Simple ways to learn one word a day.

258
00:11:23,820 --> 00:11:25,000
Ben, you go first.

259
00:11:25,640 --> 00:11:26,620
Okay, part three.

260
00:11:27,460 --> 00:11:29,780
My first idea is choosing the word, Mia.

261
00:11:30,390 --> 00:11:32,100
Because that is where people get stuck.

262
00:11:32,850 --> 00:11:35,340
"Which word?" Here is my rule.

263
00:11:36,120 --> 00:11:37,270
The word you missed.

264
00:11:38,360 --> 00:11:42,960
Yesterday, at some moment, you wanted to say something and could not.

265
00:11:43,020 --> 00:11:45,470
Like me, in the shop, pointing at the rice.

266
00:11:46,320 --> 00:11:47,440
That is today's word.

267
00:11:47,940 --> 00:11:50,040
The word you missed, Ben.

268
00:11:50,050 --> 00:11:50,990
Not from a list.

269
00:11:51,270 --> 00:11:52,060
From your day.

270
00:11:52,850 --> 00:11:54,860
The list gives you words you might need.

271
00:11:55,330 --> 00:11:58,100
The day gives you words you did need.

272
00:11:58,140 --> 00:12:01,000
And a word you needed has a story.

273
00:12:01,009 --> 00:12:03,040
"Shelf" is not a word on a list.

274
00:12:03,580 --> 00:12:05,760
It is the moment in the shop when you pointed.

275
00:12:06,460 --> 00:12:07,740
You will remember the shop.

276
00:12:08,400 --> 00:12:10,040
My shelf has glue, Mia.

277
00:12:10,920 --> 00:12:11,800
That is the word.

278
00:12:12,620 --> 00:12:12,940
Glue.

279
00:12:13,880 --> 00:12:15,780
A word from a list has no glue.

280
00:12:16,720 --> 00:12:18,280
A word from a shop has glue.

281
00:12:19,320 --> 00:12:21,840
So what do you do with the word, after you choose it?

282
00:12:22,680 --> 00:12:23,520
A card, Ben.

283
00:12:23,870 --> 00:12:24,880
My second idea.

284
00:12:25,480 --> 00:12:27,540
One small card, in the pocket.

285
00:12:28,150 --> 00:12:28,860
"Estante.

286
00:12:29,580 --> 00:12:32,400
Shelf." And the "again" part.

287
00:12:33,080 --> 00:12:35,200
I look at the card five times that day.

288
00:12:35,820 --> 00:12:36,560
Not for long.

289
00:12:37,040 --> 00:12:37,700
Two seconds.

290
00:12:38,260 --> 00:12:38,960
On the bus.

291
00:12:39,360 --> 00:12:39,980
At lunch.

292
00:12:40,420 --> 00:12:41,240
Before bed.

293
00:12:42,020 --> 00:12:43,640
And the next day, a new card.

294
00:12:44,160 --> 00:12:46,900
But the old card stays in the pocket for a week.

295
00:12:47,920 --> 00:12:51,060
So the old words come again by accident, Mia.

296
00:12:51,120 --> 00:12:56,240
Your hand finds last Tuesday's card while looking for today's, and you remember last Tuesday's word.

297
00:12:57,200 --> 00:12:58,940
You made your pocket into the shop.

298
00:12:59,620 --> 00:13:03,880
The pocket says the word again, the way life said your two hundred words again.

299
00:13:05,020 --> 00:13:08,540
"The pocket says the word again." Yes, Ben.

300
00:13:09,000 --> 00:13:11,380
By accident, but not by accident.

301
00:13:11,960 --> 00:13:12,740
And your version?

302
00:13:13,480 --> 00:13:14,680
You do not use cards.

303
00:13:15,200 --> 00:13:16,120
What do you use?

304
00:13:17,240 --> 00:13:18,280
I use a wall, Mia.

305
00:13:18,780 --> 00:13:19,660
My second idea.

306
00:13:20,340 --> 00:13:23,500
One word a day, on a small paper, on the kitchen wall.

307
00:13:24,220 --> 00:13:25,760
And the paper stays for a month.

308
00:13:26,499 --> 00:13:29,500
So every morning, with my coffee, I see thirty words.

309
00:13:30,260 --> 00:13:31,620
I do not study them.

310
00:13:31,660 --> 00:13:32,860
I see them.

311
00:13:32,900 --> 00:13:33,640
Thirty seconds.

312
00:13:34,280 --> 00:13:36,960
And they come again, every day, without trying.

313
00:13:38,360 --> 00:13:40,540
Thirty words on a kitchen wall, Ben.

314
00:13:41,160 --> 00:13:41,820
And your wife?

315
00:13:42,300 --> 00:13:43,280
Does she read them too?

316
00:13:44,840 --> 00:13:46,840
She learns Spanish from my wall now, Mia.

317
00:13:47,340 --> 00:13:48,120
Without trying.

318
00:13:48,780 --> 00:13:51,480
She says "estante" when she cannot find the rice.

319
00:13:52,420 --> 00:13:54,120
That is the wall.

320
00:13:54,180 --> 00:13:55,100
Nobody studies it.

321
00:13:55,840 --> 00:13:57,120
Everybody remembers it.

322
00:13:57,870 --> 00:14:00,410
The wall does the again for the whole kitchen.

323
00:14:01,770 --> 00:14:05,060
"Without trying." Ben, that is the key.

324
00:14:05,600 --> 00:14:08,880
The fifty-a-night method is all trying, and no again.

325
00:14:09,420 --> 00:14:13,120
And the wall is no trying, and all again.

326
00:14:13,180 --> 00:14:15,740
And Ebbinghaus says again is the thing.

327
00:14:16,440 --> 00:14:17,500
Not trying.

328
00:14:18,800 --> 00:14:20,160
Again is the thing, Mia.

329
00:14:20,880 --> 00:14:22,540
My third idea is the mouth.

330
00:14:23,340 --> 00:14:26,330
At lunch, I take the day's word, and I use it once.

331
00:14:26,980 --> 00:14:28,640
Out loud, if I can.

332
00:14:28,700 --> 00:14:29,330
In a sentence.

333
00:14:30,080 --> 00:14:30,989
Not a good sentence.

334
00:14:31,749 --> 00:14:34,540
"The bread is on the shelf." Ugly is fine.

335
00:14:35,240 --> 00:14:35,809
But said.

336
00:14:36,560 --> 00:14:38,200
Because the mouth remembers.

337
00:14:39,160 --> 00:14:42,440
A word that is said stays longer than a word that is seen.

338
00:14:43,740 --> 00:14:45,720
The mouth has its own curve, Ben.

339
00:14:46,260 --> 00:14:48,720
There are words I can read and cannot say.

340
00:14:49,260 --> 00:14:50,559
My mouth never met them.

341
00:14:51,460 --> 00:14:54,020
Can I tell you about my worst week with the cards?

342
00:14:54,520 --> 00:14:56,060
Because I went too fast.

343
00:14:57,240 --> 00:14:58,140
Too fast, Mia?

344
00:14:58,740 --> 00:14:59,780
With one word a day?

345
00:15:00,560 --> 00:15:02,200
How do you go too fast with one?

346
00:15:03,920 --> 00:15:05,040
I got excited, Ben.

347
00:15:05,640 --> 00:15:07,380
One word felt too slow.

348
00:15:07,440 --> 00:15:09,180
So I did five a day.

349
00:15:09,220 --> 00:15:10,170
Five cards.

350
00:15:10,680 --> 00:15:16,040
And by Thursday, I had twenty cards in my pocket, and I could not remember which was which.

351
00:15:16,700 --> 00:15:17,820
The curve came back.

352
00:15:18,260 --> 00:15:21,040
The moment I went fast, the curve came back.

353
00:15:22,080 --> 00:15:28,440
"The curve comes back the moment you go fast." Mia, that is Ebbinghaus in one sentence.

354
00:15:29,220 --> 00:15:31,360
And the fear says that, too.

355
00:15:31,370 --> 00:15:32,280
"One word a day?

356
00:15:32,860 --> 00:15:37,580
I will be old before I speak English." So let me answer with numbers.

357
00:15:38,980 --> 00:15:39,800
Numbers, Ben.

358
00:15:40,180 --> 00:15:40,560
Go on.

359
00:15:41,000 --> 00:15:42,190
I am ready for the numbers.

360
00:15:42,920 --> 00:15:46,160
One word a day is three hundred and sixty-five words a year, Mia.

361
00:15:46,900 --> 00:15:48,200
Learned, not seen.

362
00:15:48,940 --> 00:15:51,920
Fifty words a night is three thousand words in two months.

363
00:15:52,630 --> 00:15:54,900
Seen, and then two hundred remembered.

364
00:15:55,760 --> 00:15:56,690
So which is faster?

365
00:15:57,260 --> 00:16:00,410
Three hundred and sixty-five learned, or two hundred remembered?

366
00:16:01,090 --> 00:16:02,220
The slow one wins.

367
00:16:03,900 --> 00:16:05,340
The slow one wins, Ben.

368
00:16:05,960 --> 00:16:07,180
And it does not stop.

369
00:16:07,680 --> 00:16:08,580
That is the other thing.

370
00:16:09,100 --> 00:16:12,500
Fifty a night stops after two months, with tears.

371
00:16:13,240 --> 00:16:16,700
One a day goes on for thirty years, like your father.

372
00:16:17,480 --> 00:16:18,260
Which is faster?

373
00:16:18,860 --> 00:16:20,300
The one that does not stop.

374
00:16:21,460 --> 00:16:30,400
"The one that does not stop." Mia, my fourth idea is for our listeners, because many of you are doing this already, without knowing.

375
00:16:30,500 --> 00:16:32,940
Every episode, we repeat five words.

376
00:16:33,860 --> 00:16:34,200
Small.

377
00:16:34,960 --> 00:16:35,240
Step.

378
00:16:36,020 --> 00:16:36,400
Morning.

379
00:16:37,120 --> 00:16:37,460
Start.

380
00:16:38,300 --> 00:16:38,580
Feel.

381
00:16:39,420 --> 00:16:41,760
And you meet them thirty times in one episode.

382
00:16:42,600 --> 00:16:43,420
That is the wall.

383
00:16:44,140 --> 00:16:45,140
That is the pocket.

384
00:16:46,140 --> 00:16:48,740
And then, next episode, again, Ben.

385
00:16:49,260 --> 00:16:50,500
On a different day.

386
00:16:50,530 --> 00:16:51,879
And the episode after that.

387
00:16:52,500 --> 00:16:54,580
So the five words are not seen.

388
00:16:55,000 --> 00:16:56,960
They are met, again and again.

389
00:16:57,660 --> 00:16:59,500
You remember them without trying.

390
00:17:00,060 --> 00:17:02,580
You are already on the slow fast road.

391
00:17:03,580 --> 00:17:05,260
Do you have a fifth idea?

392
00:17:05,300 --> 00:17:05,890
For the evening?

393
00:17:06,880 --> 00:17:07,360
Yes, Mia.

394
00:17:08,080 --> 00:17:14,500
Before bed, after the one line from our writing episode, take the day's card and look at it one last time.

395
00:17:15,200 --> 00:17:15,800
Say it once.

396
00:17:16,620 --> 00:17:17,270
And then sleep.

397
00:17:18,220 --> 00:17:20,730
And from our sleep episode, we know what happens.

398
00:17:21,390 --> 00:17:24,840
The brain moves the word from the waiting room to the long-term room.

399
00:17:25,540 --> 00:17:26,780
You remember it in the morning.

400
00:17:27,660 --> 00:17:30,320
Give the night shift the word, not the phone.

401
00:17:31,460 --> 00:17:37,720
"Give the night shift the word, not the phone." Ben, I like how the episodes connect.

402
00:17:38,380 --> 00:17:41,820
And one more idea from me, for the words that still do not stay.

403
00:17:42,260 --> 00:17:43,420
Because some do not.

404
00:17:44,100 --> 00:17:49,920
I have a Spanish word, "aunque," that I have met fifty times, and I still cannot remember it.

405
00:17:50,980 --> 00:17:51,960
Fifty times, Mia?

406
00:17:52,410 --> 00:17:53,300
And it still falls?

407
00:17:54,080 --> 00:17:55,400
What do you do with a word like that?

408
00:17:55,920 --> 00:17:56,300
Give up?

409
00:17:57,320 --> 00:17:58,700
I used to think, "See?

410
00:17:59,100 --> 00:18:05,560
My memory is bad." Now I think, "This word needs more glue." So I gave it a story.

411
00:18:06,160 --> 00:18:14,120
I wrote one silly sentence about my sister and "aunque." And now, when I see the word, I see my sister, and I remember it.

412
00:18:14,960 --> 00:18:17,780
Some words need fifty meetings and a story.

413
00:18:18,500 --> 00:18:19,200
Add glue.

414
00:18:20,480 --> 00:18:21,300
Add glue, Mia.

415
00:18:22,120 --> 00:18:25,080
And one last idea, for the people who learn with an app.

416
00:18:25,800 --> 00:18:27,760
Many apps do this well now.

417
00:18:27,800 --> 00:18:32,169
They show you the word again on day one, day three, day seven.

418
00:18:32,920 --> 00:18:34,620
That is Ebbinghaus in your phone.

419
00:18:35,340 --> 00:18:35,600
Good.

420
00:18:36,020 --> 00:18:36,380
Keep it.

421
00:18:36,940 --> 00:18:38,470
But the app remembers for you.

422
00:18:39,240 --> 00:18:41,880
And the word you needed in the shop is not in the app.

423
00:18:42,640 --> 00:18:43,920
"The app gives you again.

424
00:18:44,400 --> 00:18:49,780
The pocket gives you glue." Ben, that is exactly the difference.

425
00:18:50,240 --> 00:18:52,840
My app had "shelf" in it for two years.

426
00:18:53,120 --> 00:18:54,860
I met it again and again.

427
00:18:55,000 --> 00:18:56,400
I remembered it in the app.

428
00:18:56,900 --> 00:18:59,260
And I still could not say it in the shop.

429
00:18:59,340 --> 00:19:01,200
It was a list word.

430
00:19:01,240 --> 00:19:05,460
Then the shop happened, and it became a life word.

431
00:19:05,480 --> 00:19:06,520
A life word, Mia.

432
00:19:07,270 --> 00:19:09,200
And that is our message today.

433
00:19:09,320 --> 00:19:11,640
Seeing a word is not learning it.

434
00:19:11,650 --> 00:19:13,960
The curve takes what you see, the same night.

435
00:19:14,560 --> 00:19:16,920
Learning is meeting the word again.

436
00:19:16,980 --> 00:19:17,829
On different days.

437
00:19:18,500 --> 00:19:19,160
Five times.

438
00:19:19,920 --> 00:19:20,630
With a little glue.

439
00:19:21,570 --> 00:19:23,540
So do not learn fifty words tonight.

440
00:19:24,240 --> 00:19:24,800
Learn one.

441
00:19:25,580 --> 00:19:26,540
Put it in your pocket.

442
00:19:27,300 --> 00:19:28,380
Meet it again tomorrow.

443
00:19:29,340 --> 00:19:31,140
The slow way is the fast way.

444
00:19:32,380 --> 00:19:34,600
The slow way is the fast way.

445
00:19:35,580 --> 00:19:40,840
Ben, let's repeat today's important words, slowly, because our listeners want to remember them.

446
00:19:42,420 --> 00:19:42,820
Word.

447
00:19:43,680 --> 00:19:44,260
One word.

448
00:19:44,920 --> 00:19:45,920
The day's word.

449
00:19:47,320 --> 00:19:48,220
A word with glue.

450
00:19:49,780 --> 00:19:50,220
Learn.

451
00:19:50,960 --> 00:19:52,180
To make something yours.

452
00:19:53,520 --> 00:19:54,960
Learn one word a day.

453
00:19:56,260 --> 00:19:57,700
Seeing is not learning.

454
00:19:58,940 --> 00:19:59,320
Again.

455
00:19:59,680 --> 00:20:00,380
One more time.

456
00:20:01,600 --> 00:20:02,820
Meet the word again.

457
00:20:03,700 --> 00:20:05,100
Again is the thing.

458
00:20:06,440 --> 00:20:06,920
Remember.

459
00:20:07,840 --> 00:20:08,800
To keep in the head.

460
00:20:09,700 --> 00:20:10,640
Remember the word.

461
00:20:11,100 --> 00:20:12,520
The words remember you.

462
00:20:13,850 --> 00:20:15,100
Day.

463
00:20:15,220 --> 00:20:16,280
One word a day.

464
00:20:16,840 --> 00:20:18,060
Five different days.

465
00:20:19,680 --> 00:20:19,980
Word.

466
00:20:21,080 --> 00:20:21,400
Learn.

467
00:20:22,660 --> 00:20:23,020
Again.

468
00:20:24,360 --> 00:20:24,800
Remember.

469
00:20:26,150 --> 00:20:27,640
Day.

470
00:20:27,740 --> 00:20:30,420
Five words, thirty times, on purpose.

471
00:20:30,920 --> 00:20:32,110
And one big idea.

472
00:20:33,120 --> 00:20:34,500
Learn one word a day.

473
00:20:35,720 --> 00:20:37,880
Now, Mia, do you have a question for our listeners?

474
00:20:38,960 --> 00:20:39,500
Yes, Ben.

475
00:20:39,960 --> 00:20:42,020
Everyone listening, here is my question.

476
00:20:42,620 --> 00:20:43,950
What is your word for today?

477
00:20:44,370 --> 00:20:47,300
The one you missed yesterday, or one from this episode.

478
00:20:47,880 --> 00:20:50,390
Write it in the comments in one short sentence.

479
00:20:50,949 --> 00:20:56,549
"My word today is shelf." And then, tomorrow, come back and write it again.

480
00:20:57,060 --> 00:20:58,100
We will see it twice.

481
00:20:58,500 --> 00:20:59,800
That is two meetings.

482
00:21:01,000 --> 00:21:01,820
Two meetings, Mia.

483
00:21:02,609 --> 00:21:05,860
And if this episode helped you, please subscribe to Two Cups English.

484
00:21:06,480 --> 00:21:09,200
We make a new episode every few days.

485
00:21:09,280 --> 00:21:13,440
Slow English, simple words, and one true story each time.

486
00:21:14,400 --> 00:21:16,690
The full text of this episode is in the description.

487
00:21:17,420 --> 00:21:20,400
Read it once now, and once next week.

488
00:21:20,440 --> 00:21:22,560
That is two meetings with every word in it.

489
00:21:23,640 --> 00:21:25,520
Thank you for spending this time with us.

490
00:21:25,940 --> 00:21:27,120
Maybe you are on the bus.

491
00:21:27,640 --> 00:21:28,720
Maybe you have a pen.

492
00:21:29,360 --> 00:21:30,420
Choose one word.

493
00:21:30,920 --> 00:21:31,880
Write it on something.

494
00:21:32,400 --> 00:21:33,340
Put it in your pocket.

495
00:21:33,630 --> 00:21:35,060
And meet it again tonight.

496
00:21:35,600 --> 00:21:36,220
That is all.

497
00:21:36,820 --> 00:21:37,560
Not fifty.

498
00:21:38,260 --> 00:21:38,520
One.

499
00:21:39,300 --> 00:21:40,840
Tomorrow, another one.

500
00:21:41,400 --> 00:21:43,040
In a year, a language.

501
00:21:44,080 --> 00:21:44,740
This is Ben.

502
00:21:45,460 --> 00:21:46,299
And this is Mia.

503
00:21:47,200 --> 00:21:48,600
This was Two Cups English.

504
00:21:49,140 --> 00:21:50,160
One word a day.

505
00:21:50,940 --> 00:21:51,919
We will see you next time.

